        "title": "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "060c20fc-1491-5624-9b50-89ab0dfe4d22",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $G(t)={e}^{t}\\,i-t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,k$\n- **II.** $H(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **III.** $K(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
