        ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M(x,y,z)=\u27e8z-{x}^{2},3x+{z}^{2},\\frac{1}{x+y}\u27e9$\n- **II.** $N(x,y)=\u27e8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+1,1-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\u27e9$\n- **III.** $P(t)=\u27e8{t}^{3},\\sqrt{1-t}\\,\u27e9$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $M(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x+y\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $P(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
